{"type": "final", "turn": 6, "content": "\n\nI compared both 2025 PA Karst predictions (same area, same period) using the Studio pair-comparison over their shared extent (~10,091 km\u00b2), sampling a 6\u00d76 grid:\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Models | KarstBinary \u2192 `sample_karst_score` [0\u20131]; KarstNumber \u2192 `sample_number` [0.2\u20131.2] |\n| Cells compared | 25 of 36 (11 dropped as no-data in either map) |\n| Correlation | \u22120.0172 |\n\n**Where they differ:** they measure *different quantities* \u2014 a karst presence score vs. a numeric karst value \u2014 so the only meaningful cross-statistic is correlation, and it's essentially zero: the two maps do not rise and fall together anywhere on the sampled grid. No difference/RMSE/agreement figure is computed because mixing the two quantities would be invalid.\n\n**Which is right:** I can't say, and no comparison can. There's no ground-truth labels for this area, so this is model-vs-model agreement, not accuracy \u2014 without labels it's unresolvable. If you want a definitive answer, the path forward is a labeled review set: I can plan a label sample on either result (`olmoearth_plan_label_sample`) so N field checks yield an honest error rate, then `olmoearth_classification_metrics` against those labels would finally show which model is closer to truth. Want me to set that up?", "forced_by_turn_cap": false, "grounding_revised": false, "seq": 14, "t": 144.828, "seconds": 3.181}
